<article class="py-16">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <p class="text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider text-sm mb-3">The Workplace · 2025</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl tracking-tight uppercase font-bold text-white mb-6" style="letter-spacing: 0.1em;">The Safeway Strike</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-12">I went on strike with my union at Safeway in 2025. I played music on the picket line, and I made a video with AI that moved through the workforce before the ratification vote. When the new contract arrived, almost nobody could tell what was in it. Everything I have built since comes out of that gap.</p>

    <figure class="mb-16">
      <img src="/images/strike/picket-01.webp" alt="Inside the store during the 2025 Safeway contract fight" class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl shadow-lg">
      <figcaption class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-3">The store floor, where a contract question gets asked and answered in about thirty seconds.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">The line as an information environment</h2>
    <div class="md:flex md:gap-8 md:items-start mb-6">
      <div class="md:flex-1">
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A picket line is usually described as a labor action. It is also, for the length of the strike, the fastest information network a workforce has. People who never speak inside the store talk for hours outside it. Rumors about the state of negotiations arrive, get argued over, and either survive or die. Music is part of that: it is what keeps a line together long enough for the talking to happen.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400">I brought a speaker and wrote songs for it — titles that came out of what people were already saying to each other on the line. The connections I made there, with members and with local leadership who walked with us, are the reason a pilot later got approved. That order matters, and it is the part most technology write-ups leave out.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="mt-8 md:mt-0 md:w-64 shrink-0">
        <img src="/images/strike/picket-02.webp" alt="On the line with a speaker and a picket sign" class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl shadow-lg">
        <figcaption class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-3">The sound system, 2025.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">Before the vote, I used generative tools to make a video that spread among workers. It was persuasive, it was fast, and it was made by one member with a laptop. I have no interest in pretending that was a neutral act. It is the same capability that makes management's messaging effective, pointed the other way — and taking it seriously as a method means taking seriously what it does when it is pointed back.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">What happened after the settlement</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">We won a new contract, and then nobody knew what it said. The pay rates were hard to find. The vacation changes sat between red outlines. Management could frame the terms however it liked, because workers had few practical ways to check.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">The asymmetry is structural rather than personal. An employer maintains a legal department, algorithmic scheduling and HR analytics. A worker gets a seventy-page contract in the mail that most never read. Whoever shapes that information environment shapes whether rights get exercised or forfeited by default — and the effect is worst during a contract transition, when old norms linger and new terms get described selectively.</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-16">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-bg-card)] border border-[var(--color-border)] p-6 rounded-lg">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-lg uppercase tracking-wide text-white mb-2">Where KARL came from</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">After the strike I worked with Mike, a former aerospace engineer turned produce clerk. Between picking tomatoes and melons I learned retrieval architectures from him. Out of that came KARL: an open-source assistant that answers contract questions with citations to the article and section it read them from.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-bg-card)] border border-[var(--color-border)] p-6 rounded-lg">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-lg uppercase tracking-wide text-white mb-2">The hypothesis I had to give up</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">I assumed workplace misinformation was adversarial — that managers deliberately mislead. But managers have been open to KARL, which suggests much of it is unintentional: a structural product of who has access to institutional knowledge, not a conspiracy. That distinction is now the research question rather than the premise.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">The study this turns into</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The workplace is a laboratory of information flow that computational sociology has barely touched, and unions are the institution built to correct it democratically. The design is comparative: establish a misinformation baseline through interviews at every level of a store — workers, stewards, managers, store director — before the tool enters the environment, then deploy, then measure what changed, then repeat at a second store and a second company for cross-company comparison.</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-6">
      <li>Does the tool change steward confidence and accuracy?</li>
      <li>Where does human mediation help, and where does it become a barrier?</li>
      <li>Are contract errors random or patterned — and do they cluster around particular provisions, and consistently favor one side?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The uncomfortable methodological question is the telemetry one: what counts as surveillance when you apply quantitative methods to a tool that workers are supposed to control? Measuring a deployment means instrumenting it, and instrumenting a workforce is precisely the thing the tool refuses to do for employers. The current answer is that research runs on voluntary steward interviews and surveys, and that no individual query is recorded — which costs analytic power, deliberately.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">The risk that matters most is a worker being hurt by a bad retrieval: a wrong wage rate or a fabricated citation could cost someone pay or a job. High-stakes lookups bypass the language model entirely and resolve from structured tables, and every answer carries a citation a steward can check against the physical contract.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide">The songs</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Thirty-odd recordings came out of the strike and the weeks around it, including <em>Check Your Receipt</em>, <em>Clock In, Clock Out (Riot Mix)</em>, <em>Buy One, Break One (Deal With It)</em>, <em>Milk for the Monarchy</em>, <em>Listen Up, Albertsons!</em>, <em>Eight Dollars an Hour</em> and <em>This Little Wage of Mine</em>. A separate piece, <em>Worker-Controlled AI Tools Augmenting Contract Literacy</em>, is the research written as music rather than about it.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">The recordings are being prepared for publication here; until they are, this page names them rather than linking to them.</p>

